AD  - Ordos Institute of Liaoning Technical University, Ordos, 017004, China
AB  - Intelligent mine ventilation systems are critical infrastructures for the intelligent transformation of mining operations. They are moving toward adaptive optimization of ventilation networks in complex environments through real-time multi-source sensing and algorithm-driven decision support; fully autonomous, safety-certified closed-loop control remains a long-term goal. Traditional ventilation systems, constrained by static design and passive response mechanisms, often show low resilience and slow recovery in emergencies such as gas outbursts and roadway collapses, which can accelerate accident escalation and increase risks to personnel and equipment. Drawing on resilience paradigms from power systems and urban infrastructure, this study proposes a “Resilient Mine Ventilation Network”. Based on a physical-information-application three-layer architecture, the framework enhances disturbance resistance, functional survivability, and post-event recoverability. By integrating fluid-dynamics models with deep learning and digital twin technologies, we establish a five-module framework encompassing perception, instability grading, modeling, assessment, and enhancement, together with uncertainty-aware decision support. The proposed framework aims to provide a theoretically grounded and practically implementable pathway for ventilation safety in highly dynamic and strongly coupled underground mining environments. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.
