AB  - The safety performance of the numerous high-rise building structures of China has a significant impact on urban security. To address the challenges in evaluating engineering design verification and in-service performance of high-rise building structures，this study integrates structural monitoring，finite element model updating，and building information modeling（BIM）technologies to establish a digital twin model for high-rise building structures. A method for engineering design verification and in-service performance evaluation of high-rise buildings was proposed，with a particular focus on engineering design parameters such as the inter-story drift ratio specified in the design codes. Using in-service state information including stress，natural frequency，and mode shapes obtained from an IoT-based monitoring system for high-rise buildings，the multi-scale finite element model of the structure was updated. The updated model was then used to recalculate the engineering design parameters that reflect the true in-service performance of the structure，thereby achieving engineering design verification and in-service performance evaluation. The results were mapped to a lightweight BIM model constructed using WebGL technology. A case study was conducted based on the 300-meter-plus Dabaihui Plaza high-rise building and dynamic parameter identification was achieved based on the proposed monitoring IoT. The relative errors of local stress between the established and updated models were all within 10%，and the relative error of characteristic frequency was 5. 9%. Furthermore，recalculation and visualization of engineering parameters were implemented based on WebGL，preliminarily validating the effectiveness of the proposed engineering verification and in-service performance evaluation method. The results demonstrate that the recalculated engineering parameters of the Dabaihui Plaza high-rise building under typhoon and earthquake actions，obtained using the proposed evaluation model，comply with the requirements of the relevant Chinese codes，thereby effectively assessing the structural safety performance. © 2025 Editorial Office of Journal of Building Structures. All rights reserved.
