AB  - Report. In modern construction, the technology of building information modeling (BIM) has proven its effectiveness in optimizing design work and increasing the level of automation of processes. However, in the mining industry, the potential of BIM technologies remains unrealized due to the specific features of mining operations, including their scale, complexity and heterogeneity of geological conditions. The purpose of the study. The main purpose of the work is to analyze the possibilities of implementing BIM technologies in the mining industry, with a special focus on developing an information model of a mine (MIM). The research is aimed at bridging the existing technological gap in the process of digital transformation of the industry. Scientific novelty. The innovative approach is to create a fully automated workflow for generating MIM based on parametric modeling. The developed technique makes it possible to transform the raw mining data into an informative model that takes into account the characteristics of the surrounding rocks. Research methodology. In the course of the work, an automated process for creating models of underground workings using parametric modeling was developed. The methodological base includes: the analysis of source data (point clouds, tunnel grid models, information about wells), the use of parametric objects based on topology, and the use of scripts to manage modeling rules. The results of the study. The conducted research has confirmed the possibility of effectively creating MIM for mining operations using parametric objects and scripts. The results obtained open up new prospects for the development of the MineBIM concept, which includes: machine control systems, automation of production processes, and the development of digital twins. It is expected that further implementation of BIM technologies in the mining industry will lead to a significant increase in production efficiency, work safety, and environmental sustainability of the industry. © 2025, North Caucasian Institute of Mining and Metallurgy, State Technological University. All rights reserved.
