AD  - School of Civil and Transportation Engineering, Beijing University of Civil Engineering and Architecture, No. 1 Zhanlanguan Road, Beijing, 100044, China
AB  - Vibration accidents of high-rise buildings are frequently reported around the globe and the downtime of high-rise buildings for accident investigation can lead to substantial social and economic losses for stakeholders. This research developed an edge-cloud collaborative framework for vibration anomaly detection and vibration source localization. Firstly, a high-precision vibration sensor with wireless time synchronization and edge computing capability is introduced. Secondly, a vibration anomaly detection algorithm is proposed based on edge computing of sensor nodes. Thirdly, a vibration source localization algorithm is proposed based on cloud computing of a structural dynamics digital twin model. Finally, the effectiveness of the proposed methods is validated through a case study of a 17-story high-rise building, and the results indicate that vibration anomaly events can be reliably detected and localized. The outcomes of this study are expected to provide a useful reference for vibration anomaly management of high-rise buildings. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
