AD  - Shanghai Construction Grp Co Ltd, 666 Dongdaming Road, Shanghai, 200080, China
AB  - High-speed railway (HSR) bridges are critical transportation infrastructure operating under stringent constraints (e.g., zero tolerance for operational disruptions, limited maintenance windows) and demanding real-time safety monitoring. Traditional train load identification methods suffer from complex modeling, poor anti-noise performance, and inadequate real-time applicability, failing to balance accuracy, feasibility, and timeliness for HSR scenarios. To address this gap, this study proposes a novel train gross weight identification method based on bridge strain data: an Integral Mean of Strain (IMS) index is introduced to suppress transient interference and simplify the load–strain relationship, with a derived theoretical IMS-gross weight correlation that avoids cumbersome parameter calibration. Validated via numerical simulations (multiple bridge/train types, 10–50 dB noise) and two-week real-bridge tests, results show identification errors ≤ 1% (simulations) and ≤ 5% (field tests). The method's key innovation lies in its lightweight, real-time online capability (low computational complexity, fast response), meeting digital twin systems’ low-latency data needs—critical for HSR bridges’ dynamic physical-virtual mapping and real-time health assessment. It thus provides vital data support for digital twin-based monitoring systems, with broad potential in intelligent transportation infrastructure. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd
